    jgWhyBtn: 'Why?', jgWhyRespaldo: 'Backing:', jgWhyFuente: 'Source:',
    jgWhyNivel_estudio: 'published study.', jgWhyNivel_guia: 'technical guide from a university.',
    jgWhyNivel_practica: 'growers’ practice taught by the platform (no outside source checked yet).',
    jgWhyNivel_simple: 'game rule (a simplification so the game can be played).',
    jgWhyN_flip: 'Flowering too early', jgWhyN_evento: 'Unsolved problem', jgWhyN_muerta: 'Wilted plant', jgWhyN_sinDato: 'No data',
    jgWhy_fotoperiodo: 'With 18 hours of light the plant keeps growing in the vegetative stage; with 12 hours of light and 12 of darkness it starts to flower. That is what the University of Guelph study did.',
    jgWhy_intensidad: 'Each stage needs a different intensity. In flowering, more light gave a bigger harvest in the University of Guelph study. The ranges for each stage are the platform’s.',
    jgWhy_riego: 'With the growing medium always soaked, the roots run out of air, root fungi appear and the plant can wilt. With very little water the plant is stressed.',
    jgWhy_nutrientes: 'Without enough nitrogen in the vegetative stage, the older leaves turn yellow starting at the tip. The game’s nutrient range comes from the platform.',
    jgWhy_temperatura: 'The platform recommends a mild daytime temperature. Spider mites thrive in heat and dry air.',
    jgWhy_humedad: 'High humidity brings fungi: powdery mildew on the leaves and, in flowering, bud rot. The Utah State guide advises keeping relative humidity below half in flowering.',
    jgWhy_flip: 'Switching to 12/12 too soon makes a still small plant flower. The minimum vegetative weeks are a game rule taken from the platform’s calendar.',
    jgWhy_evento: 'An unsolved problem keeps harming the plant every week. How much it takes away is a game rule.',
    jgWhy_oidio: 'Powdery mildew: white, dust-like spots on the leaves. It is slowed by leaving space between plants so air can flow and by lowering the humidity.',
    jgWhy_botrytis: 'Botrytis: the bud rots from the inside when humidity stays high during flowering. It is prevented with low humidity and good air circulation.',
    jgWhy_acaros: 'Spider mites: they thrive in heat and low humidity; they leave small pale dots on the leaves and fine webbing. The natural-predator answer comes from the platform.',
    jgWhy_carencia_n: 'Nitrogen deficiency: the yellowing starts on the older leaves, from the tip towards the centre.',
    jgWhy_exceso_riego: 'Overwatering: the roots run out of air, root fungi appear and the plant wilts. The fix is good drainage and letting it dry between waterings.',
    jgWhy_muerta: 'The game’s health reached zero because of the mistakes that added up. That health is a game number, but in real life problems such as overwatering can indeed kill a plant.',
    jgWhy_tricomas: 'Harvesting by trichome colour is a growers’ practice taught by the platform; we did not find a study that confirms it. The points for each colour are a game rule.',
    jgWhy_secado: 'Drying slowly in a cool, dark place with the air aimed at the wall is the practice the platform teaches. Its figures have no checked outside source yet.',
    jgWhy_curado: 'Curing in glass jars, opening them daily in the first week and for several weeks, is the practice the platform teaches, with no checked outside source yet.',
    jgWhy_sinDato: 'This value has no approved figure yet, so it neither adds nor takes away.',
    jgGuia_secado: 'Drying: the buds hang in a cool, dark room with medium humidity, with the air moving without blowing straight at them. It is done when a small stem snaps instead of bending (the snap test).',
    jgGuia_curado: 'Curing: the dry buds go into airtight glass jars with controlled humidity. In the first week the jars are opened every day to let moisture out, and curing lasts several weeks.',
